= Appendix G — Model Answers and the Half-Answer

These brief models demonstrate how to connect a definition to a concrete example. They are study exercises, not purported past questions.

== G.1 Data and Information

*Prompt:* Distinguish data from information using examination marks.

*Half-answer:* “Information is data.”

*Model:* Data are raw facts or symbols in the context being studied; the unprocessed marks 35, 48 and 57 are data. Information is data interpreted usefully; their calculated mean of approximately 46.7 summarises the marks and is information for this task.

== G.2 Input and Output

*Prompt:* Explain why a touchscreen can be both an input and an output device.

*Half-answer:* “It is a screen.”

*Model:* When the screen displays a menu it presents output. When it detects a user's touch and passes the selection to the system, it accepts input. The same device performs both functions.

== G.3 RAM and Storage

*Prompt:* Explain why saving a file matters before a power cut.

*Half-answer:* “RAM is fast.”

*Model:* Ordinary RAM is volatile, so unsaved changes held only in working memory can be lost when power is removed. Saving writes the changes to a chosen persistent destination, such as an SSD. A separate backup is still needed to protect against loss of that destination.

== G.4 Converting Binary

*Prompt:* Convert binary 1011 to decimal and show the place values.

*Half-answer:* “Eleven.”

*Model:* The weights from left to right are 8, 4, 2 and 1. Therefore 1 × 8 + 0 × 4 + 1 × 2 + 1 × 1 = 11 in decimal. Writing the weights makes the result checkable.

== G.5 System and Application Software

*Prompt:* Distinguish an operating system from a spreadsheet.

*Half-answer:* “Both are programs.”

*Model:* An operating system manages resources such as memory, processes and files and provides services to applications. A spreadsheet is application software used for tasks such as organising values in cells and calculating with formulas. The spreadsheet runs with support from the operating system.

== G.6 Spreadsheet References

*Prompt:* Explain how to keep a tax-rate reference fixed when copying a formula.

*Half-answer:* “Copy the formula.”

*Model:* In common spreadsheet notation, write the rate's cell as `$D$1`. The dollar signs fix both column D and row 1 when the formula is copied. A relative reference D1 would normally shift with the copy.

== G.7 UPS and Backup

*Prompt:* Explain why a UPS and a backup are not interchangeable.

*Half-answer:* “Both protect a computer.”

*Model:* A UPS can provide temporary battery power during an outage, within its rating, allowing time to save work or shut down. A backup is a separate recoverable copy of data. A UPS cannot by itself recover a file after theft or storage failure.

== G.8 Internet and Web

*Prompt:* Distinguish the internet from the World Wide Web.

*Half-answer:* “They are both online.”

*Model:* The internet is a worldwide network of interconnected networks. The web is a system of linked resources that uses the internet, commonly through HTTP or HTTPS. Email is another example of a service that can use internet connectivity.
